    "result_text": "`AcceptanceTest` is the only test class in the suite, and it passes. Done.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the `ageBand` derivation with minimal structural impact:\n\n- **`AgeBand` util (new, 28 lines)** \u2014 a small, cohesive `AgeBand.of(birthDate, registrationDate)` returning `MINOR`/`ADULT`/`SENIOR` (or `null` when no birth date). This follows the existing `util` pattern (`Household`, `CustomerCode`, `CheckDigit`) so the derivation logic lives in a class with essentially zero surrounding complexity, rather than being grown into `Owner` or the mapper.\n- **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added `birthDate` (input) to `OwnerFields` and `ageBand` (readOnly) to `Owner`, so the generated DTOs carry both fields.\n- **`Owner.java`** \u2014 added a `birthDate` field with plain getter/setter (each CC 1) and a `@Column(name = \"birth_date\")` so JPA persists it for the read-back on `GET`.\n- **`OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 one `@Mapping` line delegating `ageBand` to `AgeBand.of(...)`. MapStruct maps `birthDate` automatically by name; the interface method itself gains no branching.\n- **`db/hsqldb/schema.sql`** \u2014 added the `birth_date DATE` column (the profile tests use runs `ddl-auto=none`, so the column must exist in the script).\n\nNo existing method's complexity was increased; the branching lives entirely in the new standalone util. Full suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 1/1 passing, including the new `coreDerivesAgeBand` check expecting `ADULT` for a 1990 birth date against a 2026 registration date).",
